<p>Cumin is India's second-largest spice export by value after dried red chilli, and the destination pattern echoes chilli closely: China leads by a clear margin. Calendar 2024 figures show China importing USD 140.7 million of Indian cumin, more than 50% ahead of the second-place market, Bangladesh (USD 91.7 million), out of USD 784.9 million in total Indian cumin exports worldwide across roughly 237,241 MT. The driver is China's scale of food-processing and culinary demand — cumin is a staple in specific regional cuisines and in broader food manufacturing — combined with India's established cleaning, sorting and export-documentation capacity. This guide is for Chinese importers and food manufacturers needing consistent, specification-driven volume.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<p>Bangladesh, the UAE and the United States round out India's top cumin markets in the data above, each at meaningfully smaller scale than China but still significant volumes in their own right. If China isn't your specific market, see our broader <a href="/products/spices/">spices category page</a> for the full picture across destinations.</p>

<!-- insight:buyer-segmentation -->
<h2>Do Chinese buyers pay more or less per kilo than other cumin markets?</h2>
<p>Less. Dividing the WITS/UN Comtrade 2024 value by tonnage for each market gives an average unit value, and China sits near the bottom of India's top ten:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Market (2024)</th><th scope="col">Tonnes</th><th scope="col">Average unit value (USD/kg)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>China</td><td>45,803</td><td>3.07</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bangladesh</td><td>29,749</td><td>3.08</td></tr>
<tr><td>United Arab Emirates</td><td>28,919</td><td>3.13</td></tr>
<tr><td>United States</td><td>16,700</td><td>4.01</td></tr>
<tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>5,310</td><td>5.03</td></tr>
<tr><td>India, all markets</td><td>237,241</td><td>3.31</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>China took 19.3% of India's cumin by weight but 17.9% by value, so it paid about 7% below the world average. The US paid about 30% more per kilo than China, and the UK about 64% more. A unit value is an average over every contract, not a price, but the pattern is clear. China buys large lots of whole seed for processing, re-cleaning and grinding, and buys them on price. The US and UK pay for steam-sterilised, tightly specified and often smaller lots that carry more processing cost.</p>
<p>Scenario: a Qingdao seasoning manufacturer that grinds its own blends buys machine-clean 98% whole seed in full containers and does its own final cleaning. A Shanghai importer packing retail jars buys sortex 99.5% because it has no cleaning line. They are in different segments and should not benchmark against the same offer.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>What specification should a Chinese cumin order state?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — whole seed or ground; whole retains aroma longer and is the more common export form</li>
  <li><strong>Cleaning grade and purity</strong> — machine-clean 98% min or sortex 99.5% min, with admixture of other seeds and foreign matter stated as a maximum</li>
  <li><strong>Singles or splits</strong> — whole seeds versus broken seeds, which grind the same but trade cheaper</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — affects storage stability and shelf life</li>
  <li><strong>Volatile oil content</strong> — the aroma-intensity parameter that matters most to food manufacturers</li>
  <li><strong>Residues and microbiology</strong> — the pesticide residue panel tested against GB 2763, China's national standard for maximum residue limits (MRLs), plus salmonella if your customer requires it</li>
</ul>
<p>Specify these clearly and we'll quote against them rather than a generic "export quality" tier.</p>

<h2>Where is Indian cumin grown, and when does new crop arrive?</h2>
<p>Cumin is grown predominantly in Gujarat and Rajasthan, with Unjha — Asia's largest cumin trading market — functioning as the central price-discovery and processing hub. It is a rabi (winter) crop, sown after the monsoon and harvested February through April, so new-crop arrivals at Unjha build from March. Well-dried cumin stores for many months without significant quality loss, which supports year-round supply from stock rather than a narrow seasonal window.</p>

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>How much cumin fits in a 20ft container to Shanghai or Qingdao?</h2>
<p>About 13-15 MT bagged in a 20ft and 23-25 MT in a 40ft. Cumin is a light seed, so the box fills by volume before it reaches the weight limit, which is why the 40ft carries far more. At the midpoints, 24 MT against 14 MT, a 40ft holds about 71% more cumin. A 40ft is the cheaper box per tonne whenever its freight rate to Shanghai or Qingdao is less than about 1.7 times the 20ft rate, which it usually is. Divide each quoted rate by its tonnage before you choose.</p>
<p>The cargo value tells you how much a freight move matters. At China's 2024 average of USD 3.07/kg, a 14 MT 20ft carries about USD 43,000 of cumin and a 24 MT 40ft about USD 73,700. China's 45,803 MT in 2024 equals roughly 1,900 forty-foot loads. For a first order, a 20ft keeps the trial lot and the cash at risk smaller.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>Which quality checks should you request before shipment?</h2>
<p>Beyond purity and volatile oil content, ask about admixture screening for other similar-looking seeds, which can be an adulteration risk on cheaper offers in this category generally. A reputable supplier should be able to describe their cleaning and sorting process specifically, not just assert "premium quality" without detail. We can walk you through our sourcing and processing steps for cumin specifically if that level of detail is useful for your own supplier qualification process.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<!-- insight:policy-timeline -->
<p><strong>What changed for GACC registration when Decree 280 replaced Decree 248?</strong> The registration requirement itself did not go away. Decree 248 applied from 1 January 2022 and required overseas food manufacturers to register with China Customs (GACC) through the CIFER system. Decree 280 replaced it on 1 June 2026. Registrations made under Decree 248 remain valid, and CIFER is still the system. Under Decree 280, registrations run for five years, and manufacturers not renewed automatically can apply between 3 and 12 months before expiry, according to a Singapore Food Agency trade circular on the change. Scenario: a Chinese importer qualifying a new Indian cumin source in October 2026 should check the manufacturer's CIFER record and its expiry date, not ask whether it was "registered under 248", and should plan any renewal at least 3 months before expiry. At the port, the goods are inspected by GACC's customs inspection teams, still widely called CIQ by importers although CIQ merged into customs in 2018.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

