<h2>India vs. Syria and Turkey — competing cumin origins</h2>
<p>India is the dominant global cumin producer, but Syria and Turkey remain established alternative origins, particularly for buyers who've historically sourced from the Middle East. The comparison below is about origin characteristics, not a claim that one is simply better.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Factor</th><th scope="col">India</th><th scope="col">Syria / Turkey</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Production scale</td><td>World's largest producer and exporter by a wide margin</td><td>Smaller production base, more exposed to regional supply disruption</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grading system</td><td>Established machine-clean / sortex-clean grading, widely understood by buyers</td><td>Varies more by individual supplier and season</td></tr>
<tr><td>Consistency of supply</td><td>Large aggregated crop base (Gujarat/Rajasthan) supports consistent year-round export volume</td><td>More exposed to single-region weather and political-supply-chain risk</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical buyer profile</td><td>Large-volume, specification-driven food manufacturers and re-exporters (matches China's profile)</td><td>Smaller or regionally-focused buyers, sometimes at a price discount when available</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Where Syria and Turkey can win: a different harvest calendar from India's February-April rabi crop, so a second origin can cover the months when Unjha stocks are old and prices firm, and a discount when their crops are good. Where India wins: one origin can supply 45,803 MT to China in a single year, as it did in 2024, with a grading system Chinese buyers already use. Scenario: a large Chinese processor keeps India as its base supply and buys a second origin opportunistically, rather than splitting its volume evenly.</p>
</div>
